WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.407 --> 00:00:05.250
I can't believe he's doing this.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.260
He's going to go up
against an enormous army

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:09.200
of charging knights on his own?

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:10.941
You're gonna die.

00:00:10.941 --> 00:00:12.990
Hello, everyone. My name
is Roel Konijnendijk.

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:15.630
I teach ancient history at
the University of Oxford,

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.610
and I'm back now to
look at some more scenes

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:20.144
from movies and TV and
judge how real they are.

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:28.347
This is 13 years after
"Saving Private Ryan,"

00:00:28.347 --> 00:00:29.437
and we're still just like,

00:00:29.437 --> 00:00:31.320
"No, that's how you do an invasion."

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.690
This is a modern landing
craft that they've dressed up

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:35.850
to look vaguely medieval-ish.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:37.773
This actually happened, in the sense that

00:00:37.773 --> 00:00:39.900
a French army landed on English soil

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.700
during the period of the Barons' revolt.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:44.280
But it wouldn't have landed
in these landing craft,

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:45.750
which are specifically designed

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:47.940
to support a modern amphibious landing.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:50.006
And, in fact, historically,
this landing was unopposed.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.470
If the enemy's landing
on a beach like this,

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:57.120
there's only one way up the cliffs.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:58.890
Why would you go down
there and fight them?

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:00.390
You just block off that ramp

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:02.250
and watch them starve to death or go home.

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.066
It's your choice, really.

00:01:04.066 --> 00:01:06.172
You don't have to fight.
You don't run any risks.

00:01:06.172 --> 00:01:07.244
It's better for everybody.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.200
These archers have a
completely impossible range.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.150
They're firing from the cliff tops,

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:19.680
which are hundreds of meters away,

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.630
and they're firing up into the air as well

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:22.770
to get maximum range,

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:24.690
which, yes, it makes
your arrows go further,

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:26.190
but it also means that when they land,

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:27.840
they're basically just falling.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:28.980
They have no force.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:30.240
So if you hold your shield up,

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.723
they're just going to ping
off your armor or your shield.

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:36.240
Now, these guys, again,

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:38.340
are doing everything they
can to lose this battle.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:40.590
You see a bunch of horsemen
charging down at you,

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:42.360
and instead of bundling together,

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:43.980
you form a sort of loose line

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:46.740
that the horses can
actually get in between you,

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:48.960
and you kneel down for no apparent reason.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.700
No. This is where you
clump together, right?

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.400
You form shields, you get as
close together as you can.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:54.780
You stick your spears out.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:56.280
The horses can't touch you.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.590
They don't want to get
in so close to an object

00:01:58.590 --> 00:01:59.665
that they can't get through,

00:01:59.665 --> 00:02:00.873
so they're not going to charge in.

00:02:00.873 --> 00:02:02.520
And then you have the range advantage,

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:03.690
'cause you have a spear

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:05.970
and the guy on the horse
seems to just have a hammer.

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:07.200
So you're just going to poke them.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.550
I would give it a 3 out of 10.

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:10.750
The landing craft are
obviously wildly unhistorical.

00:02:19.642 --> 00:02:21.450
I mean, movies always
love to use fire, right?

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:22.980
It's going to be very spectacular.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:24.930
But obviously this would've
been very rarely used,

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:26.760
especially when you're
defending your own village.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:28.869
You're not going to set it on fire.

00:02:31.913 --> 00:02:33.180
Defenses are often built to try

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:34.350
and pin the enemy in place.

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:35.975
You surround them from the walls,

00:02:35.975 --> 00:02:37.416
you can fire down at them.

00:02:37.416 --> 00:02:39.360
Now, this town obviously
has no defenses at all,

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:40.620
which makes it more difficult.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:42.785
Blockading the enemy,
pinning them in place,

00:02:42.785 --> 00:02:44.370
that's when they're at your mercy.

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:46.328
They did sort of portray that pretty well.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:56.130
Ambushing with archers is
usually quite effective.

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:58.260
You try to hide these
people, and then they pop out

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:00.840
when the enemy's most blocked and confused

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:02.280
and doesn't know where they're going.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:04.770
But we shouldn't overestimate
how lethal that would be.

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:06.510
Unless you have a lot of archers,

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:09.120
the enemy's soon going
to be able to regain

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.070
enough organization to
throw their shields up,

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:13.440
and then your arrows are not
going to be very effective.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:15.630
Unless you have overwhelming numbers.

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:17.400
You wouldn't necessarily
try to defend a village

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.647
that had no fortifications,
but if you had to,

00:03:19.647 --> 00:03:20.996
yeah, you would get up on the roof.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:28.170
The idea that the orcs can
actually fire back at them

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:30.690
and kill some of them,
that's very unrealistic,

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:32.048
because the villagers are going to be

00:03:32.048 --> 00:03:33.139
completely in the dark.

00:03:33.139 --> 00:03:35.130
The light is down there,
where the fire is.

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:36.120
As far as the orcs can tell,

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:38.070
the arrows are coming from the night.

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:40.620
You'd do some of these things,
like funneling the enemy,

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:41.760
trying to block their way,

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:43.500
trying to hit them when they're confused

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:44.610
or when they're distracted.

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:46.519
We'd give this maybe a 4 out of 10.

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:52.110
The really cool thing about this scene

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:54.510
is they have a ditch. And
you can see how it works,

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:57.300
'cause they have to go into
the ditch to approach the wall,

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.820
and it means that the wall
is very high all of a sudden.

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:01.680
You can see this ditch forming a part

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:03.363
of an integrated defensive system.

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:09.270
The fighting for the ladders

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:11.010
and trying to make your way onto the walls

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:13.210
and all of that, broadly
speaking, is accurate.

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:17.730
There would be a lot of engineering.

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:19.350
There would be a lot of times in history

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:22.089
when siege warfare is
a place where a lot of

00:04:22.089 --> 00:04:24.664
technological innovation
happens in a very short time.

00:04:24.664 --> 00:04:26.190
And you have famous instances,

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:27.693
like when the Romans attacked

00:04:27.693 --> 00:04:29.940
this Greek city of Syracuse in Sicily.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:32.130
Archimedes is one of the defenders,

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:35.160
and he supposedly came up with
all these different devices.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:38.730
They were so new and
innovative and strange

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:41.610
that the Romans supposedly
towards the end of this siege

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:43.710
became so afraid of these devices

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:46.680
that if they just saw a bit of
rope dangling over the wall,

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:49.230
they would run away
because they were terrified

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:52.410
that Archimedes had some new
devilry to throw at them.

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:54.990
So this idea of using strange machines

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:57.240
in the defense of a city is historical.

00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:59.010
It's just that these particular things

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:00.330
that they've come up with here,

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:01.710
they literally don't do anything

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:03.518
that a thrown rock couldn't do.

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:09.738
These balls just make no sense.

00:05:10.821 --> 00:05:12.030
Well, the marble chutes, and when you know

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:14.040
where the balls come out,
you just step aside, right?

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:15.210
If you get hit by these balls,

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:16.950
you have no one to blame but yourself.

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:18.270
It has some things right.

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:20.010
You gotta give points for a good ditch.

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:21.710
I give it a 5 out of 10.

00:05:25.157 --> 00:05:27.570
Soldier: We were ordered by
Pierre to hold this bridge.

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:29.310
There is a siege of Limoges,

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:31.200
which they've turned into
the battle of Limoges

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:33.480
for some inexplicable reason.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:35.460
You can see the city
burning in the background.

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:36.930
This was a complete English victory.

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:39.360
They just overcame a small
garrison in this city

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:40.710
and then murdered everyone.

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:42.270
But there was no battle outside the walls

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:43.145
or anything like that.

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:49.350
So, they're trying to tempt
the French into attacking

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:51.660
by killing hostages in France with them.

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:54.630
Now in the Hundred Years' War,
it was a fairly common thing

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:56.520
for the English to
provoke the French knights

00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:57.353
as a way to win.

00:05:57.353 --> 00:05:59.490
So several of their most famous victories,

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:02.430
at Crécy, Poitiers,
and arguably Agincourt,

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:05.070
the French knights charged
when they shouldn't have,

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:06.900
and this caused chaos.

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:08.616
They charged into prepared positions,

00:06:08.616 --> 00:06:10.470
they charged into disadvantageous terrain,

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:11.550
all these kinds of things.

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:13.008
So the English clearly thought of this

00:06:13.008 --> 00:06:14.224
as a great way to win,

00:06:14.224 --> 00:06:16.560
and I think they were just
trying to transpose this

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:18.600
into the story of the siege of Limoges,

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:20.133
but it doesn't belong there.

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:24.139
That one guy who just runs out,

00:06:24.139 --> 00:06:25.440
like, what are you going to do?

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:27.360
Like, my guy, don't.

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:29.358
He's going to go up
against an enormous army

00:06:29.358 --> 00:06:31.290
of charging knights on his own?

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:32.524
You're gonna die.

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:34.710
They're saying at the beginning,

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:36.600
"We have orders to defend this bridge."

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:37.770
Strategically senseless,

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:39.420
because you can just ford the river.

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:41.730
So if they're trying to
stop the English advancing

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:43.800
across the river, which is
what I guess you're doing

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:45.150
when you're defending a bridge,

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:47.640
the English can just walk across.

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:48.840
They don't form a formation.

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:50.190
They don't have archer support.

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:52.710
They don't seem to have
any cavalry nearby.

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:54.581
They're all going to die, I'm afraid.

00:06:54.581 --> 00:06:55.856
Shall we give it a 2 out of 10,

00:06:55.856 --> 00:06:57.739
because they've got the date right?

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:05.790
These are supposed to be
these wolfskin warriors,

00:07:05.790 --> 00:07:09.150
these Úlfhéðnar. Sometimes
they're also called Berserkers.

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:11.340
The idea that there
are these wild warriors

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:13.110
who work themselves up into a frenzy

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:14.820
and then go into battle basically naked

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:17.970
with a complete disregard
for their own safety.

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:19.860
But actually, when you read the sources

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:22.500
about these warriors, they're
just ordinary soldiers

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:23.640
in the sense of their equipment.

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:25.950
They wear shields, clothes, armor,

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:27.810
and they go into battle fighting as a unit

00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:30.390
that behaves, as far as
we can tell, normally.

00:07:30.390 --> 00:07:32.280
It's just very much later sources,

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:34.650
centuries after these guys were around,

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:36.930
start embellishing this story.

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:39.388
As far as we know, that never happened.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:48.300
The spear throwing is actually something

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:49.290
that's very realistic.

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:51.000
They would throw their spears in battle,

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:53.100
but then, of course, they
would fight in close combat,

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:55.320
wearing armor, helmets
and swords and shields,

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:56.220
for the most part.

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:05.190
You can climb a basic fortification.

00:08:05.190 --> 00:08:07.920
If it's a simple fort made
of wood, in particular,

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:09.690
you can either throw
a ladder up against it

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:10.950
or climb it even by hand,

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:12.300
and this does sometimes happen.

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:13.920
If the enemy's not very prepared for you,

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:15.960
you might be able to sneak in that way.

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:19.235
But if it's defended at all,
this is going to go very badly.

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:26.700
If these defenders had any sense at all,

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:28.770
they would be forming a formation

00:08:28.770 --> 00:08:30.390
in the streets of this little town,

00:08:30.390 --> 00:08:32.363
and these invaders would have to

00:08:32.363 --> 00:08:33.630
try and break that formation,

00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:35.460
which they don't seem
very well equipped to do.

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:37.680
They don't have any armor.
They don't have shields left.

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:38.910
They just have their little axes.

00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:40.920
Well, in close combat against the spear,

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:43.410
you're going to be at a
massive reach disadvantage.

00:08:43.410 --> 00:08:45.150
Viking raids were obviously endemic

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:47.001
in Europe in this
period. Most of the time,

00:08:47.001 --> 00:08:49.710
Vikings would target obviously
more vulnerable places.

00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:53.100
It's just not a very profitable
arrangement, or undertaking,

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:55.830
to try and attack a town that's defended.

00:08:55.830 --> 00:08:59.550
So you'd go after isolated
farmsteads, monasteries,

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:00.962
perhaps settlements that didn't have

00:09:00.962 --> 00:09:02.670
any kind of wall or palisade even,

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:04.500
so that you could actually just walk in

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:05.760
and take what you wanted.

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:07.680
In terms of the actual
fighting that we're seeing,

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:09.247
this is 3 out of 10.

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:14.430
Actually, that marching scene
has a very nice depiction

00:09:14.430 --> 00:09:17.520
of the fact that marching order
would be relatively loose.

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:19.470
You wouldn't march in very tight order,

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:21.090
because you'd just get
in each other's way.

00:09:21.090 --> 00:09:22.290
When they would be marching,

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:24.570
there might be as much as
2 meters between each man.

00:09:24.570 --> 00:09:26.100
So they're showing that really well.

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:27.607
Soldier: And this

00:09:27.607 --> 00:09:30.750
is the Roman army amid
our forests and swamps.

00:09:30.750 --> 00:09:31.950
It's always very tempting, I think,

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:34.500
for movie makers to have
these battle planners

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:36.450
either sketch it out in the sand

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:39.200
or to have them pore over
a battle map and plan.

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:42.210
Maps and the kind of top-down
visualization of the world

00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:44.910
that we are used to just
wasn't common back then.

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:46.350
You would try and find a high point

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:47.910
to overlook the battlefield, maybe,

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:49.290
so that you would have the overview.

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:50.340
But generally speaking,

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:53.880
you wouldn't rely on this
kind of abstract visualization

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:55.650
to understand the space.

00:09:55.650 --> 00:09:57.330
Soldier: When we cut off the head,

00:09:57.330 --> 00:09:59.580
the tail won't notice,

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:01.290
and we hack it to bits.

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:02.967
Fundamentally, you don't need to split up

00:10:02.967 --> 00:10:04.016
this army even further.

00:10:04.016 --> 00:10:06.570
And because they're strung
out along a narrow road,

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:08.220
they can't support each other anyway.

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:09.955
The front of this column is going to be

00:10:09.955 --> 00:10:11.430
miles away from the back of it.

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:13.335
You're already in a position where you can

00:10:13.335 --> 00:10:14.700
essentially attack them with impunity.

00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:16.875
You don't have to do
anything to these poor trees.

00:10:20.490 --> 00:10:22.230
This is the battle of
the Teutoburg Forest.

00:10:22.230 --> 00:10:25.140
So, this is a real event in
which three Roman legions

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:28.350
were destroyed by an
alliance of Germanic tribes.

00:10:28.350 --> 00:10:30.540
They were essentially drawn into a forest

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:31.710
where they did not know the paths

00:10:31.710 --> 00:10:32.970
and they did not know the way,

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:34.770
and then they were
attacked from all sides.

00:10:34.770 --> 00:10:37.350
And so fighting them when
they don't expect you,

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:38.183
great tactic.

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:47.610
In the accounts of battle that we have,

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:50.250
it was raining, so good
luck with making a big fire

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:51.360
in the middle of this field.

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:54.330
There was a huge downpour,
which distressed the Romans

00:10:54.330 --> 00:10:56.670
because it made the ground
all slick and slippery.

00:10:56.670 --> 00:10:59.940
It ruined their visibility.
It soaked their equipment.

00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:01.860
They weren't able to operate well

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:03.450
because it was raining all the time.

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:05.940
They're using it to try and
split up this formation.

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:07.200
But, again, there's no point.

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:09.090
If you're coming at them from all sides,

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:12.030
you're pushing them back
into their own formation,

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:13.860
and the closer they get together,

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:16.320
the harder it'll be for them to maneuver,

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:18.300
to fight, to get organized.

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:19.500
That's how you beat them.

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:21.780
So making big lines of fire in between

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:23.820
to separate them actually
works against you,

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:25.110
because then they have nowhere to go

00:11:25.110 --> 00:11:26.550
but directly into you,

00:11:26.550 --> 00:11:28.140
and they're going to fight all the harder.

00:11:28.140 --> 00:11:29.640
So this is actually a terrible idea,

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:31.170
but obviously it looks
really good on-screen,

00:11:31.170 --> 00:11:32.280
so that's what we're going with.

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:33.955
I think it deserves an 8 out of 10.

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:40.080
Why not either let the archers handle it

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:42.810
or send out a formation of guys?

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:44.940
Let him try and break a
shield wall on his own

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:46.380
with only a little sword.

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:48.222
Like, he's not gonna have a good time.

00:11:53.550 --> 00:11:55.470
Obviously, every time he has
to fight someone, he stops.

00:11:55.470 --> 00:11:57.390
That's when he would get shot,

00:11:57.390 --> 00:12:00.003
and he has to do that several
times and then find cover,

00:12:00.003 --> 00:12:01.740
which just happens to be there.

00:12:01.740 --> 00:12:02.970
He's immensely lucky.

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:05.910
He's basically wearing sort
of premium-grade plot armor.

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:08.820
Also, I'm begging movie
makers to please stop it

00:12:08.820 --> 00:12:10.717
with the orders to the archers.

00:12:10.717 --> 00:12:12.270
This never happened.

00:12:12.270 --> 00:12:14.160
The "nock, draw, loose,"
thing, it's not real.

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:16.118
Don't let anyone convince you otherwise.

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:21.360
The Crabfeeder's men, the men in yellow,

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:23.520
they actually have these
swords and bucklers,

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:26.280
which is a very particular style of combat

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:27.630
where they have these curved swords

00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:29.100
and these very small shields.

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:30.510
Usually they would've been made of metal,

00:12:30.510 --> 00:12:31.590
and you can actually use them

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:33.600
both to guard yourself and to attack.

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:35.610
So they have a very
particular style of armor

00:12:35.610 --> 00:12:37.635
that you don't see in
movies or TV series a lot.

00:12:42.810 --> 00:12:45.420
In terms of the fighting, it's just chaos.

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:48.390
Everybody's just running
around engaging the enemy

00:12:48.390 --> 00:12:50.700
one at a time, hacking
at anything they can see.

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:51.900
It's very exciting to watch,

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:54.540
but this is not a battle,
this is just a big mess.

00:12:54.540 --> 00:12:56.250
2 out of 10.

00:12:56.250 --> 00:12:57.750
Soldier: Tighten the circle!

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:03.120
These kind of protective
circular formations are attested,

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:05.550
though you do get Romans
sometimes forming a circle.

00:13:05.550 --> 00:13:07.320
But having them be in a single line

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:08.610
makes it much too vulnerable.

00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:11.340
If even one of these men dies,
the entire circle collapses.

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:13.890
The enemy will be inside, and
no one will have any defense.

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:16.590
You have to make that into
a multi-rank formation.

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:18.124
Otherwise, it's completely useless.

00:13:23.130 --> 00:13:25.285
They just throw themselves at these Romans

00:13:25.285 --> 00:13:27.000
like a zombie horde or something.

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:29.610
It's kind of sad to see
this kind of portrayal

00:13:29.610 --> 00:13:32.340
of northwest European
peoples, essentially.

00:13:32.340 --> 00:13:36.540
There's this tendency to turn
actual well-equipped fighters

00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:39.480
into these wild shirtless barbarians,

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:40.650
because all the way back

00:13:40.650 --> 00:13:42.270
to essentially Greek and Roman times,

00:13:42.270 --> 00:13:44.760
we have this stereotype that
the people from the north,

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:46.650
people from the forests of Europe

00:13:46.650 --> 00:13:49.320
are these sort of barely
civilized wild men.

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:51.060
They knew how to equip themselves,

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:53.160
and wealthy warriors in
all of these societies

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:54.678
would've been just as well equipped

00:13:54.678 --> 00:13:55.978
as the Romans in this scene.

00:14:03.630 --> 00:14:05.040
So, they are confusing two things.

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:07.140
They're confusing chariots
and scythed chariots,

00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:09.180
which are different kinds
of weapons essentially.

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:12.270
Chariots were still in
use among the most wealthy

00:14:12.270 --> 00:14:14.520
sort of warlords in
Britannia at this point.

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:16.380
But these would be missile platforms.

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:18.900
So on those chariots, you
would be firing arrows

00:14:18.900 --> 00:14:20.820
or throwing javelins, for instance.

00:14:20.820 --> 00:14:22.980
You wouldn't be engaging
them in close combat,

00:14:22.980 --> 00:14:26.771
because that would be a terrible
waste of your poor ponies.

00:14:26.771 --> 00:14:28.350
These men would be heavily armored,

00:14:28.350 --> 00:14:29.940
not like you're seeing in this fragment.

00:14:29.940 --> 00:14:31.800
They would be carrying
mail shirts themselves.

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:32.850
They would be carrying helmets.

00:14:32.850 --> 00:14:34.740
Some centuries before this,

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:36.570
there had been an attempt to create

00:14:36.570 --> 00:14:38.070
this kind of armored battering ram

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:39.390
in the form of the scythed chariot.

00:14:39.390 --> 00:14:42.780
So you attach scythe blades to
the wheels of these chariots

00:14:42.780 --> 00:14:44.730
in order to try and send them

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:46.950
at enemy formations to break them up.

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:49.770
This is something that we first
see in the Persian empire.

00:14:49.770 --> 00:14:51.480
But it never works very well.

00:14:51.480 --> 00:14:54.300
It's very easy to either
get out of the way

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:55.620
or dispatch the riders

00:14:55.620 --> 00:14:58.860
and then essentially
nullify this entire device.

00:14:58.860 --> 00:14:59.825
4 out of 10.

00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:04.022
Fire on my command.

00:15:04.022 --> 00:15:05.005
Ready.

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:07.980
Arrow loops are a way to make sure

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:09.960
that you can project missiles

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:11.940
from the wall while you are under cover.

00:15:11.940 --> 00:15:14.730
So enemies will find it
very difficult to fire

00:15:14.730 --> 00:15:15.810
into those loops.

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:17.760
That's why they always have
this sort of wedge shape.

00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:21.027
They will let you cover a
wide range of territory.

00:15:28.050 --> 00:15:30.200
These guys have been here,
according to the story,

00:15:30.200 --> 00:15:32.580
for 10,000 years, defending this gap.

00:15:32.580 --> 00:15:33.420
I don't know what they did.

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:35.940
Like, they built the most
simple wall they could

00:15:35.940 --> 00:15:37.260
for about a week, and then they,

00:15:37.260 --> 00:15:38.940
I guess, did crossword puzzles.

00:15:38.940 --> 00:15:40.950
This is incredibly primitive,

00:15:40.950 --> 00:15:43.080
in terms of what it's trying to achieve

00:15:43.080 --> 00:15:44.910
as a defensive fortification.

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:46.650
It's just a single wall.

00:15:46.650 --> 00:15:48.810
There's nothing you can
do about them climbing up,

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:51.540
breaking in, and getting to where you are.

00:15:51.540 --> 00:15:53.310
Make sure the walls have bastions

00:15:53.310 --> 00:15:55.530
so that you can cover
the foot of the wall,

00:15:55.530 --> 00:15:57.930
so that you can defend
yourself against an enemy

00:15:57.930 --> 00:15:59.130
that actually reaches you.

00:15:59.130 --> 00:16:02.010
The actual scene they
show us is a 1 out of 10.

00:16:02.010 --> 00:16:03.277
Absolutely worthless.

00:16:07.530 --> 00:16:09.633
Always looking for the crossbowmen,
and they're never there.

00:16:09.633 --> 00:16:12.330
There's, like, one guy,
I think, among the French

00:16:12.330 --> 00:16:14.550
outside the walls who has a crossbow.

00:16:14.550 --> 00:16:16.500
But these should be quite
prestigious warriors.

00:16:16.500 --> 00:16:18.480
The French's answer to the longbow

00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:21.030
is to have these armored
crossbowmen show up in battle.

00:16:21.030 --> 00:16:21.863
They treasure them a lot,

00:16:21.863 --> 00:16:23.730
and they spend a lot of time
training as crossbowmen,

00:16:23.730 --> 00:16:26.640
and some of the wealthiest city dwellers

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:29.331
would equip themselves as
crossbowmen for these fights.

00:16:32.995 --> 00:16:34.650
You also have pavises,

00:16:34.650 --> 00:16:36.810
which are basically great standing shields

00:16:36.810 --> 00:16:39.690
that are used as cover mostly
for crossbowmen or archers.

00:16:39.690 --> 00:16:41.580
It's basically just a door
that you're carrying around

00:16:41.580 --> 00:16:42.660
and you set down in front of you

00:16:42.660 --> 00:16:45.030
so that arrows will get stuck
in the board, basically.

00:16:45.030 --> 00:16:46.890
So they're using those to
try and defend themselves,

00:16:46.890 --> 00:16:50.007
but then they stand way behind
it, which is great, I guess.

00:16:50.007 --> 00:16:51.548
Like, why did you bother then?

00:16:54.655 --> 00:16:56.610
No one's wearing helmets,
which is also great.

00:16:56.610 --> 00:16:57.443
You can always say, like,

00:16:57.443 --> 00:16:59.310
"Oh, we want to see the actors' faces."

00:16:59.310 --> 00:17:00.600
There are arrows flying everywhere.

00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:03.039
You want to protect your head.

00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:08.793
Soldier: Light!

00:17:09.906 --> 00:17:10.793
Recede!

00:17:12.026 --> 00:17:13.560
The gunpowder use is so strange.

00:17:13.560 --> 00:17:15.450
It was known in Europe at this time,

00:17:15.450 --> 00:17:17.280
and, again, we're talking about a siege,

00:17:17.280 --> 00:17:20.340
so maybe they were incentivized
to try and use tricks.

00:17:20.340 --> 00:17:21.900
Guns aren't known yet in this period.

00:17:21.900 --> 00:17:24.150
The earliest gunpowder weapons,

00:17:24.150 --> 00:17:25.620
these cannon that shoot, again,

00:17:25.620 --> 00:17:27.810
like, big arrow-shaped projectiles,

00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:30.090
occur several decades later.

00:17:30.090 --> 00:17:31.680
So I have no idea what
they're trying to do here,

00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:33.600
but I guess it's because,
"Well, they had gunpowder,

00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:35.220
so let's use gunpowder

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:37.350
because then we can show
big explosions and fire,

00:17:37.350 --> 00:17:38.430
and we always want that."

00:17:38.430 --> 00:17:39.840
It has nothing to do with history.

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:40.950
This kind of weapon isn't real,

00:17:40.950 --> 00:17:42.420
and in fact this fight didn't happen.

00:17:42.420 --> 00:17:43.920
2 out of 10.

00:17:43.920 --> 00:17:46.050
My favorite depiction of
ancient warfare in movies

00:17:46.050 --> 00:17:49.110
is the scene of the battle
of Gaugamela in "Alexander,"

00:17:49.110 --> 00:17:51.450
which is not a very good
movie, but the battle at least

00:17:51.450 --> 00:17:53.220
is depicted in a way that makes sense,

00:17:53.220 --> 00:17:55.500
where you can see the formations
behaving as they should.

00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:57.600
You can see each unit
equipped as it should be.

00:17:57.600 --> 00:18:00.180
So that, I feel, is a
really faithful adaptation,

00:18:00.180 --> 00:18:01.620
a way that you can actually see

00:18:01.620 --> 00:18:02.910
what this might have looked like.

00:18:02.910 --> 00:18:03.900
Thanks very much for watching.

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:06.713
If you enjoyed this video, why
not click on the one above?

